<%# Budget Form Partial - matches Categories design patterns

    MODE-AWARE, and it has to be. A rule belongs to a category OR a pool (Budget#exactly_one_owner)
    and this one form serves both, but until the Budget page existed nothing linked a pool-mode
    rule here, so the form only ever spoke about categories. It offered a pool-mode rule a category
    picker — a control whose every option makes the record invalid ("cannot belong to both") — and
    called the pool's own funding rule a spending limit. Now that §8's page links every rule to
    this form, that is user-reachable, so each mode is asked about the owner it actually has.

    THE THIRD MODE IS A PROPOSAL (§8's suggestion panel, amendment A). Accepting a dated bill or a
    rate needs an envelope the user does not have yet, the category re-pointed at it, and the rule
    written — so the form grew an OPTIONAL ENVELOPE HALF rather than the flow growing a second
    controller. One form, one POST, one transaction: BudgetsController#create hands both halves to
    BudgetProposal and a failure anywhere writes nothing.

    The pool branch omits `prorated` deliberately: it spreads a monthly category cap across the
    days of the month (CategoryCalculator#prorated_active?) and nothing reads it for a pool-mode
    rule, so offering it would be a switch that does nothing. %>
<% pool_mode = @budget.pool_mode? %>
<% proposal = @envelope.present? %>
<%# THE SCHEDULE IS ONLY EDITABLE ON THE WAY IN. A rule's shape (basis, interval, anchor, item) is
    measured by the engine and submitted back; changing it later is the edit form's business, and
    an existing rule's shape is not this partial's to re-offer. %>
<% new_pool_rule = @budget.new_record? && (pool_mode || proposal) %>
<% subtitle = "Set spending limits for #{@budget.category&.name || "this category"}" %>
<% subtitle = "How #{@budget.pool.name} gets filled each period" if pool_mode %>
<% subtitle = "A new #{@envelope.name} envelope, and the rule that fills it" if proposal %>
<% cancel_path = @budget.category ? category_path(@budget.category) : categories_path %>
<% cancel_path = budget_page_path if pool_mode || proposal %>
<%= page_header(title: @budget.persisted? ? "Edit Budget" : "New Budget", subtitle: subtitle) %>

<div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
  <%= simple_form_for @budget, html: { class: "space-y-6" } do |f| %>
    <%= f.error_notification %>
    <%= f.error_notification message: f.object.errors[:base].to_sentence if f.object.errors[:base].present? %>

    <%# Main Form Card %>
    <div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded border border-gray-100 p-6">
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <% if proposal %>
          <%# THE ENVELOPE HALF. Editable, because both fields can legitimately need changing: the
              proposed name is the CATEGORY's name and the user may already have a pool called
              that (Pool validates name uniqueness per user), and `account_id` is read from
              `users.default_account_id`, which is nullable — a user who has nominated no account
              must be ASKED for one rather than have a blank submitted on their behalf, since
              `require_account_for_budget_pools` refuses an account-less budget pool outright.

              `envelope[category_id]` is hidden and is NOT the rule's owner: it is the category to
              be re-pointed at the new envelope. See BudgetsController#set_envelope for why it
              cannot travel under the form's own `category_id`. %>
          <div>
            <%= label_tag "envelope_name", "New envelope", class: "form-label" %>
            <%= text_field_tag "envelope[name]", @envelope.name, id: "envelope_name", class: "form-input" %>
            <p class="form-hint">
              Accepting this points <%= @envelope.category.name %> at this envelope, so all
              <%= @envelope.category.name %> spending comes out of it from now on.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <%= label_tag "envelope_account_id", "Funded from", class: "form-label" %>
            <%= select_tag "envelope[account_id]",
                           options_from_collection_for_select(
                             current_user.pools.accounts.order(:name), :id, :name, @envelope.account&.id
                           ),
                           include_blank: "Select an account",
                           id: "envelope_account_id",
                           class: "form-input" %>
            <p class="form-hint">The account this envelope is filled from when you distribute a paycheck.</p>
          </div>

          <%= hidden_field_tag "envelope[category_id]", @envelope.category.id %>
        <% elsif pool_mode %>
          <%# THE FORM DOES NOT OFFER THE POOL, which is not the same as the pool being
              unwritable: `budget_params` permits `pool_id`, so a tampered submission DOES
              re-parent the rule — within the user's own pools and nowhere else, because
              `scoped_owner` looks it up through `current_user.pools` and a stranger's id 404s.
              Both directions are pinned in spec/requests/budgets_spec.rb.

              No picker because the page that lists rules groups them BY pool, so moving one
              between envelopes is a re-parenting that list should own rather than a control
              buried in one rule's form. The field is submitted back so the record keeps the
              owner it arrived with. %>
          <div>
            <label class="form-label">Pool</label>
            <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
              <%= @budget.pool.name %>
            </div>
            <%= f.hidden_field :pool_id %>
          </div>
        <% elsif @budget.category_id %>
          <%# Show category name for existing budgets %>
          <div>
            <label class="form-label">Category</label>
            <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
              <%= @budget.category.name %>
            </div>
            <%= f.hidden_field :category_id %>
          </div>
        <% else %>
          <%# Category Selection (only for new budgets without category_id) %>
          <div>
            <%= f.association :category,
                collection: current_user.categories.expenses.budgetable.order(:name),
                label_method: :name,
                value_method: :id,
                include_blank: "Select an expense category",
                label: "Category" %>
          </div>
        <% end %>

        <%# Budget Amount %>
        <div>
          <%= f.input :amount,
              as: :decimal,
              label: pool_mode || proposal ? "Rule Amount" : "Budget Amount",
              input_html: {
                placeholder: "0.00",
                step: 0.01,
                min: 0
              },
              hint: pool_mode || proposal ? "What this rule asks for #{budget_rule_basis(@budget)}." :
                "Enter the maximum amount you want to spend in this category" %>

          <%# WHAT THE RULE SAYS TODAY, beside what a drift suggestion is proposing it should say.
              The prefilled figure is in THE RULE'S OWN UNIT — SuggestionEngine inverts
              `Budget#steady_ask` before putting it on the wire, precisely so that nothing here
              converts it — and the label above already names that unit, so the two figures on
              screen are directly comparable. Shown only when they differ: on every other edit the
              field IS the current amount and repeating it would be one number printed twice. %>
          <% if @current_amount.present? && @budget.amount.to_d != @current_amount.to_d %>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500" data-current-amount>
              Currently <%= number_to_currency(@current_amount) %> <%= budget_rule_basis(@budget) %>.
            </p>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <% if new_pool_rule %>
          <%# THE SHAPE OF THE RULE, measured by the suggestion engine and submitted back. `basis`
              and `anchor_date` ride hidden because neither is a decision the user is being asked
              to make here — the anchor is the bill's own next due date and the basis is which of
              the two rule shapes was detected — while the INTERVAL is offered, because a
              single-occurrence bill is proposed with a GUESSED interval and the panel says so.
              Correcting the guess in the form it came from is cheaper than writing a wrong rule
              and editing it afterwards, and it cannot produce an invalid shape: this branch only
              renders the field where an anchor exists, which is exactly the row of §3.1 that
              accepts any interval ≥ 1. %>
          <%= f.hidden_field :basis %>

          <% if @budget.anchor_date.present? %>
            <%= f.hidden_field :anchor_date %>
            <div>
              <%= f.input :interval_months,
                  as: :integer,
                  label: "Comes round every (months)",
                  input_html: { min: 1, step: 1 },
                  hint: "First due #{@budget.anchor_date.strftime("%b %-d, %Y")}. Change this if the bill runs on a different cycle." %>
            </div>
          <% else %>
            <%= f.hidden_field :interval_months %>
          <% end %>

          <% if @budget.item %>
            <%# THE ITEM THIS RULE PAYS, shown and submitted back rather than picked. It is what
                makes the rule payable at all — BudgetCalculator reads its entries to decide
                whether the bill has been settled this cycle — and it arrived from the engine,
                which chose it by measuring the item's own history. `item_id` is ownership-scoped
                on the way in (BudgetsController#scoped_owners): a stranger's id would write a
                rule against THEIR spending, so it 404s rather than rendering their item's name
                here. %>
            <div>
              <label class="form-label">Pays</label>
              <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
                <%= @budget.item.name %>
              </div>
              <%= f.hidden_field :item_id %>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>

        <% unless pool_mode || proposal %>
          <%# Prorated toggle %>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
              <%= f.check_box :prorated, class: "form-checkbox" %>
              <%= f.label :prorated, "Prorate daily", class: "form-label mb-0 cursor-pointer" %>
            </div>
            <p class="form-hint">
              Spread the budget evenly across the month so you can see if you're on pace day-to-day. Turn off for expenses that happen in one-time chunks (like rent or subscriptions).
            </p>
          </div>
        <% end %>

      </div>
    </div>

    <%# Action Buttons %>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-end gap-3">
      <%# Back where the rule is listed, which for a pool-mode rule is the Budget page and never
          the pool page — the same destination BudgetsController#owner_path sends a save to. %>
      <%= link_to "Cancel", cancel_path, class: "btn btn-secondary" %>

      <%= f.submit @budget.persisted? ? "Update Budget" : "Create Budget",
          class: "btn btn-primary" %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
